net-external-force-law-fa53d667.interpretation:
The equation 
\mathbf{F}_{ext} = \frac{d\mathbf{P}_{total}}{dt}
 relates the net external force 
\mathbf{F}_{ext}
 to the time rate of change of the total linear momentum 
\mathbf{P}_{total}
 of a system. Here, 
\mathbf{F}_{ext}
 represents the vector sum of all forces exerted on the system by agents external to the system. Internal forces, those between particles within the system, cancel out in pairs due to Newton's third law and therefore do not contribute to the net external force. The total linear momentum 
\mathbf{P}_{total}
 is the vector sum of the individual momenta of all particles comprising the system. This equation essentially extends Newton's second law from a single particle to a multi-particle system or continuous body. It implies that if the net external force on a system is zero, the total linear momentum of the system remains constant, a principle known as the Conservation of Linear Momentum.

net-external-force-law-fa53d667.limits_and_boundary:
In the limit where the net external force 
\mathbf{F}_{ext}
 is zero, the equation dictates that the time derivative of the total momentum is zero, meaning 
\mathbf{P}_{total}
 is constant. This is the statement of the Conservation of Linear Momentum for an isolated system. If the system consists of a single particle with constant mass, this equation reduces to the familiar form of Newton's second law, 
\mathbf{F} = m\mathbf{a}
, because 
\mathbf{P}_{total} = m\mathbf{v}
, and 
\frac{d(m\mathbf{v})}{dt} = m\frac{d\mathbf{v}}{dt} = m\mathbf{a}
. This law holds in both non-relativistic classical mechanics and, with the appropriate relativistic definition of momentum (
\mathbf{p} = \gamma m\mathbf{v}
), also in special relativity.

kant-cosmological-antinomies-topology-5f.interpretation:
The formula presents two opposing claims about the nature of the universe, framed within Kant's First Antinomy. The Thesis posits that the integral of time from a singularity (
$t_{\text{sing}}$
) to the present time (
$t_0$
) is finite, and the spatial volume (
$\operatorname{Vol}(\Sigma)$\n) of the universe is also finite. This suggests a bounded universe with a definite beginning. The Antithesis, conversely, asserts that the integral of time from negative infinity to the present (
$t_0$
) is infinite, implying an eternal universe, and that the spatial volume (
$\operatorname{Vol}(\Sigma)$\n) is also infinite. The integral $\int dt$ represents the total duration of time, while $\operatorname{Vol}(\Sigma)$ represents the spatial extent of the universe. The antinomy arises because both propositions can be argued for with seemingly valid reasoning, leading to a paradox.
